---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import FAQ from '../../components/FAQ.astro';

const slug = "blog/marketing-agency-valuation-multiples";
const title = "Marketing Agency Valuation Multiples: 2026 Benchmarks";
const description = "Marketing agency valuation multiples for 2026: 3x–7x EBITDA for most agencies, by size and service type, with the adjustments that raise or lower the multiple.";
const published = 'October 8, 2026';
const readTime = "7 min read";

const faqItems = [
  {
    "q": "What multiple do marketing agencies sell for?",
    "a": "Most digital marketing agencies sell for 3 to 7 times adjusted EBITDA, according to FE International (May 2026), with an overall range of 3x to 12x. Smaller agencies under $500,000 of EBITDA sell for 2.5x to 4x. Agencies above $5 million of EBITDA sell for 7x to 12x."
  },
  {
    "q": "How much is a digital marketing agency worth?",
    "a": "A digital marketing agency is worth its adjusted EBITDA multiplied by a multiple that depends on size and quality. An agency with $600,000 of adjusted EBITDA is worth about $1.8 million to $3.0 million at 3x to 5x. Revenue alone is a weak guide, with sources putting revenue multiples at 0.4x to 1.0x."
  },
  {
    "q": "What is a good EBITDA multiple for a marketing agency?",
    "a": "A good multiple is at or above the band for the agency size, such as 4x to 6.5x for $1 million to $2.5 million of EBITDA. First Page Sage reports 4.9x for digital marketing agencies with $1M to $3M of EBITDA. Multiples above 8x are usually limited to larger, specialised or high-growth agencies."
  },
  {
    "q": "What is SDE and when is it used?",
    "a": "SDE is seller's discretionary earnings: profit plus the owner's pay and perks. It is used for smaller owner-operated agencies, typically under $2 million of revenue, and sells at about 2x to 4x. Above about $500,000 of EBITDA, buyers usually switch to EBITDA multiples."
  },
  {
    "q": "Do marketing agencies sell on revenue multiples?",
    "a": "Revenue multiples are used as a sanity check rather than the main method. FE International gives 0.4x to 1.0x of trailing revenue, with the upper end for agencies with highly recurring retainer revenue. Two agencies with the same revenue can differ widely in value because profit differs."
  },
  {
    "q": "What increases the value of a marketing agency?",
    "a": "Retainer revenue above 60%, sustained growth of 15% or more, a clear specialisation, a strong leadership bench and low client concentration all raise the multiple. FE International puts the retainer premium at 1x to 2x. Documented processes that do not depend on the owner also help."
  },
  {
    "q": "What lowers the value of a marketing agency?",
    "a": "A single client above 30% to 40% of revenue lowers the multiple by 1.5x to 2x, according to FE International. Project-based revenue, owner dependence, falling margins and generalist positioning also reduce it. Fixing concentration before a sale usually adds more value than cutting costs."
  },
  {
    "q": "How accurate are published agency valuation multiples?",
    "a": "Published multiples are indicative, not audited. The publishers are mostly M&A advisers, they do not disclose deal samples, and their ranges differ, such as a blended 3x to 8x from Breakwater against 3x to 12x from FE International. Use them to bracket a range, then confirm with comparable deals or a professional valuation."
  }
];

const articleSchema = { "@type": "Article", "headline": title, "description": description, "datePublished": "2026-10-08", "dateModified": "2026-10-08", "author": { "@type": "Person", "name": "Waseem Shahzad", "url": "https://freemarketingcalculator.com/about/" }, "publisher": { "@id": "https://freemarketingcalculator.com/#org" }, "mainEntityOfPage": `https://freemarketingcalculator.com/${slug}/` };
const faqSchema = { "@type": "FAQPage", "mainEntity": faqItems.map(i => ({ "@type": "Question", "name": i.q, "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": i.a } })) };
const breadcrumbSchema = { "@type": "BreadcrumbList", "itemListElement": [
  { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 1, "name": "Home", "item": "https://freemarketingcalculator.com/" },
  { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 2, "name": "Blog", "item": "https://freemarketingcalculator.com/blog/" },
  { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 3, "name": "Marketing Agency Valuation Multiples", "item": `https://freemarketingcalculator.com/${slug}/` }
]};
---
<Base title={title} description={description} slug={slug} schema={[articleSchema, faqSchema, breadcrumbSchema]}>
  <section class="page-hero" aria-labelledby="art-h1">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="eyebrow">Valuation · {readTime}</p>
      <h1 id="art-h1">Marketing Agency Valuation Multiples: 2026 Benchmarks</h1>
      <p class="citation">Most digital marketing agencies sell for 3 to 7 times adjusted EBITDA, and larger specialised agencies reach 8 to 12 times. FE International reports 2.5x to 4x under $500,000 of EBITDA and 7x to 12x above $5 million. Size, retainer mix, growth and client concentration move the multiple.</p>
      <div class="meta-badges"><span class="badge">📅 {published}</span><span class="badge">✓ {readTime}</span></div>
      <a href="/marketing-agency-valuation-calculator/" class="btn-hero-cta">Open the Agency Valuation Calculator <svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><line x1="5" y1="12" x2="19" y2="12"></line><polyline points="12 5 19 12 12 19"></polyline></svg></a>
    </div>
  </section>
  <div class="container">
    <div class="article-layout">
      <article class="article-body prose">

        <div class="definition-box" style="margin-top: var(--space-8);">
          <div class="definition-box__label">Key Takeaways</div>
          <ul style="margin: var(--space-2) 0 0 var(--space-5); color: var(--text-mid); line-height: 1.8;">
            <li>Most digital marketing agencies sell for 3x–7x adjusted EBITDA; the overall range is 3x–12x (FE International, May 2026).</li>
            <li>Multiples rise with size: 2.5x–4x under $500,000 of EBITDA and 7x–12x above $5 million.</li>
            <li>First Page Sage reports 4.9x for digital marketing agencies with $1–3M EBITDA and 9.0x with $5–10M (Q1 2025).</li>
            <li>Retainer revenue above 60% adds 1x–2x; a client above 30–40% of revenue subtracts 1.5x–2x.</li>
            <li>Estimate a value range with the <a href="/marketing-agency-valuation-calculator/">marketing agency valuation calculator</a>, then confirm with comparable deals.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <h2 id="answer">What Multiple Do Marketing Agencies Sell For?</h2>
        <p>Most digital marketing agencies sell for roughly 3 to 7 times adjusted EBITDA, with larger and more specialised agencies reaching 8 to 12 times. <a href="https://www.feinternational.com/blog/digital-marketing-agency-valuation" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow">FE International (May 20, 2026)</a> reports 2.5 to 4 times for agencies under $500,000 of EBITDA and 7 to 12 times for those above $5 million. The multiple rises with size, recurring revenue and growth, and falls with client concentration.</p>
        <p>The free <a href="/marketing-agency-valuation-calculator/">marketing agency valuation calculator</a> applies multiples like these to revenue and EBITDA. The sections below show where the published ranges come from, how they differ and how to adjust them.</p>

        <h2 id="by-size">Agency EBITDA Multiples by Size</h2>
        <p>Agency EBITDA multiples increase with the size of the business: under $500,000 of adjusted EBITDA sells at 2.5 to 4 times, and above $5 million sells at 7 to 12 times. Buyers pay more for scale because larger agencies have deeper teams, less owner dependence and more predictable cash flow.</p>
        <div class="bench-table-wrap" style="margin: var(--space-4) 0;">
          <table class="bench-table">
            <thead><tr><th scope="col">Adjusted EBITDA</th><th scope="col">EBITDA multiple</th><th scope="col">Typical SDE multiple</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>Under $500,000</td><td>2.5x–4x</td><td>2.5x–3.5x more common at this tier</td></tr>
              <tr style="background:var(--off-white)"><td>$500,000–$1 million</td><td>3x–5x</td><td>—</td></tr>
              <tr><td>$1 million–$2.5 million</td><td>4x–6.5x</td><td>—</td></tr>
              <tr style="background:var(--off-white)"><td>$2.5 million–$5 million</td><td>5.5x–8.5x</td><td>—</td></tr>
              <tr><td>$5 million and above</td><td>7x–12x</td><td>—</td></tr>
              </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p class="bench-source">Source: <a href="https://www.feinternational.com/blog/digital-marketing-agency-valuation" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow">FE International (May 20, 2026)</a>, which states its bands are validated against PitchBook deal data and public agency comparables. Overall range: 3x to 12x, with most agencies at 3x to 7x.</p>

        <h2 id="by-type">Agency Valuation Multiples by Service Type</h2>
        <p>Multiples differ by service type: for agencies with $1 million to $3 million of EBITDA, First Page Sage reports 4.9 times for digital marketing, 5.2 times for growth marketing and 5.3 times for social media marketing. The same table shows the multiple rising to 9.0 to 10.2 times for agencies with $5 million to $10 million of EBITDA.</p>
        <div class="bench-table-wrap" style="margin: var(--space-4) 0;">
          <table class="bench-table">
            <thead><tr><th scope="col">Agency type</th><th scope="col">$1–3M EBITDA</th><th scope="col">$3–5M EBITDA</th><th scope="col">$5–10M EBITDA</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>Digital marketing</td><td>4.9x</td><td>6.1x</td><td>9.0x</td></tr>
              <tr style="background:var(--off-white)"><td>Growth marketing</td><td>5.2x</td><td>7.0x</td><td>10.2x</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Performance marketing</td><td>5.0x</td><td>6.5x</td><td>9.3x</td></tr>
              <tr style="background:var(--off-white)"><td>Social media marketing</td><td>5.3x</td><td>7.1x</td><td>9.2x</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Creative agencies</td><td>4.6x</td><td>6.9x</td><td>8.1x</td></tr>
              </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p class="bench-source">Source: First Page Sage, Q1 2025 average EBITDA multiples "based on M&amp;A deals and advisor input", as published in the <a href="https://legacyadvisors.io/2022-2025-u-s-digital-marketing-advertising-ma-report/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow">Legacy Advisors report</a>. No sample size is disclosed.</p>

        <h2 id="adjustments">What Raises or Lowers an Agency Multiple</h2>
        <p>An agency multiple moves up or down by one to two turns depending on retainer mix, growth, specialisation and client concentration. <a href="https://www.feinternational.com/blog/digital-marketing-agency-valuation" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow">FE International (May 20, 2026)</a> lists the adjustments below; they are guidance from one publisher, not a formula.</p>
        <div class="bench-table-wrap" style="margin: var(--space-4) 0;">
          <table class="bench-table">
            <thead><tr><th scope="col">Factor</th><th scope="col">Effect on EBITDA multiple</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>Retainer revenue above 60%</td><td>1x–2x higher than a project-based shop</td></tr>
              <tr style="background:var(--off-white)"><td>Demonstrated AI integration</td><td>1x–2x premium</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Sustained growth of 15%+ a year</td><td>0.75x–1.5x premium</td></tr>
              <tr style="background:var(--off-white)"><td>Strong leadership bench</td><td>0.5x–1.5x premium</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Clear specialisation</td><td>0.5x–1.5x premium</td></tr>
              <tr style="background:var(--off-white)"><td>One client above 30–40% of revenue</td><td>1.5x–2x discount</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Generalist creative shop vs specialised B2B performance agency</td><td>1.5x–2x lower at the same EBITDA</td></tr>
              </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <h2 id="examples">Agency Valuation Examples</h2>
        <p>An agency with $600,000 of adjusted EBITDA is worth about $1,800,000 to $3,000,000 at the 3x to 5x band, and an owner-operated agency with $400,000 of seller's discretionary earnings is worth $800,000 to $1,600,000 at 2x to 4x. A revenue check of 0.4x to 1.0x of trailing revenue brackets the EBITDA result, while the top of the SDE range exceeds it, so that agency should expect the lower to middle part of its range unless retainer revenue is strong.</p>
        <div class="bench-table-wrap" style="margin: var(--space-4) 0;">
          <table class="bench-table">
            <thead><tr><th scope="col">Example</th><th scope="col">Inputs</th><th scope="col">Multiple</th><th scope="col">Estimated value</th><th scope="col">Revenue check (0.4x–1.0x)</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>EBITDA method</td><td>$4,000,000 revenue, 15% margin = $600,000 EBITDA</td><td>3x–5x</td><td><strong>$1,800,000–$3,000,000</strong></td><td>$1,600,000–$4,000,000</td></tr>
              <tr style="background:var(--off-white)"><td>SDE method</td><td>$1,200,000 revenue, $400,000 SDE (owner pay plus profit)</td><td>2x–4x</td><td><strong>$800,000–$1,600,000</strong></td><td>$480,000–$1,200,000</td></tr>
              </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p class="bench-source">Inputs are illustrative. The 2x–4x SDE range and the 0.4x–1.0x revenue range come from <a href="https://www.feinternational.com/blog/digital-marketing-agency-valuation" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow">FE International (May 20, 2026)</a>; the EBITDA band comes from its $500,000–$1 million row.</p>

        <h2 id="methods">EBITDA, SDE and Revenue Methods Compared</h2>
        <p>EBITDA is the standard basis for agencies above about $500,000 of earnings, SDE is used for smaller owner-operated agencies, and revenue multiples serve only as a sanity check. Revenue multiples ignore profitability, so two agencies with the same revenue can be worth very different amounts.</p>
        <div class="bench-table-wrap" style="margin: var(--space-4) 0;">
          <table class="bench-table">
            <thead><tr><th scope="col">Method</th><th scope="col">Formula</th><th scope="col">Best for</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>EBITDA</td><td>Adjusted EBITDA × EBITDA multiple</td><td>Agencies above $500,000 of EBITDA</td></tr>
              <tr style="background:var(--off-white)"><td>SDE</td><td>Seller's discretionary earnings × SDE multiple</td><td>Owner-operated agencies, typically under $2M revenue</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Revenue</td><td>Trailing revenue × 0.4–1.0</td><td>Floor or sanity check</td></tr>
              </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p>Adjusted EBITDA adds back owner compensation above a market salary and one-off costs, then deducts a market-rate salary for the work the owner does. Buyers value the adjusted figure, so documenting the add-backs raises the credibility of the number.</p>

        <h2 id="sources">Why Published Agency Multiples Differ</h2>
        <p>Published agency multiples differ because each publisher uses a different deal sample and does not disclose it. <a href="https://www.breakwaterma.com/blog/digital-marketing-agency-valuation-multiples-2026" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow">Breakwater M&amp;A (January 2026)</a> gives a blended range of 3x to 8x, and both Breakwater and FE International cite an average multiple of about 6.46x for agencies with $2.4 million of adjusted EBITDA, attributed to unnamed industry research. The Legacy Advisors report gives 8x to 12x for best-in-class agencies without a source.</p>
        <p>None of these publishers discloses sample sizes, and several are advisers who earn fees from agency sales. Use the ranges to bracket a value, then confirm with comparable deals or a professional valuation before negotiating.</p>

